{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>which You gave us as You swore to our fathers</b> — to give us, and You fulfilled — a land flowing with milk and honey. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 26:15). If so, the scriptural parsing is: \"and the land which You gave us — a land flowing with milk and honey — as You swore to our fathers.\" And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 26:15) that its meaning is that it should remain so always, i.e., \"and bless the land which You gave us, to be a land flowing with milk and honey.\" But there is no need for any of this, for included within the utterance or the oath is the giving, as a person says to his fellow: \"You swore to me your field; you told me a maneh [a sum of money].\" And similarly: \"as YHWH, the Elohim of your fathers, spoke to you — a land flowing with milk and honey\" (Deuteronomy 6:3), and we have already explained there. And likewise: \"and He gave him a house and bread\" — He said to him, and He gave him land (1 Kings 11:18). And let it not seem difficult to you here that in the oaths to the fathers, <b>a land flowing with milk and honey</b> is not mentioned, for since that land at that time was a land flowing with milk and honey, behold He swore to them regarding a land flowing with milk and honey. Alternatively, <b>to our fathers</b> — those who came out of Egypt, for it was said to them (Exodus 3:8), \"to a land flowing with milk and honey,\" just as in the matter of \"and they did evil to us, the Egyptians and our fathers\" (Numbers 20:15)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:55:55.843610101Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}